#!/usr/bin/env bash
# consigne -- the front door for depositing prose into the Obsidian vault,
# and the only place that reports what a deposit left behind.
#
# KIND: verb
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHAT THIS IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
# ===========================================================================
#
# It is NOT a second implementation of `fonde consign`. It runs THE SAME
# mechanism -- bibliothecaire's `lib/consign-prose.sh`, itself vendored from
# basheur's `impl/consign-prose` -- located at runtime, never copied. There is
# one implementation and two doors:
#
#     fonde consign <path>...     the OLD door. Still works; says it is old.
#     consigne <path>...          this one.
#
# Zach, 2026-08-10: "make consigne equivalent or backwards compatible with
# fonde consign and have fonde consign loudly point to consigne as the new
# correct approach." The equivalence is literal and mechanical, not a promise
# in prose: `fonde`'s own do_consign runs
#
#     bash "$ROOT/lib/consign-prose.sh" "$VAULT" "$@"
#
# and so does this, with the same $VAULT resolved from the same environment
# variable and the same default. Same argv, same deposit, same exit codes.
# bin/tests/consigne.test.sh case B pins that call shape against a recording
# stub, so the two doors cannot drift into two behaviours without a red suite.
#
# A SECOND COPY WAS THE OBVIOUS DESIGN AND IT IS THE WRONG ONE. `bin/fonde`
# says so in its own header, about itself: "If a contract store returns to
# PATH ... this file holds a SECOND COPY of an impl that store also holds, and
# two copies drift." The first draft of this verb (hf7y/realisateur#121, commit
# b81de52) did exactly that -- it reimplemented the deposit as a flat `cp` plus
# a `git commit`, with no frontmatter, no provenance, no read-back gate and no
# overwrite refusal. It was not backwards compatible with `fonde consign` in
# any respect: different argv, different vault layout, different exit codes,
# and a note the DUPLICATED report below cannot even see, because that report
# reads the `source_sha256` the real mechanism writes and the flat copy does
# not. That draft is what this replaces.
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL, IF IT ONLY FORWARDS
# ===========================================================================
#
# Because the deposit is HALF a reaping pass, and the other half has never
# been visible anywhere. hf7y/scheduler#86, measured 2026-08-10:
#
#     2,391 lines sitting in the repo and in the vault at once, byte-for-byte.
#     All deposited by one commit -- fbdbae9, 2026-08-01. Nine days later
#     every one of them is still here.
#
# `consign-prose.sh` is explicit that it deletes nothing; it deposits, then
# reports what is now safe to remove. That is the right call -- removal needs a
# judgement the depositor cannot make. The defect is that NOTHING CONSUMES THAT
# REPORT. It is printed once, to a terminal, and then the queue it describes
# does not exist anywhere. `fauche` refuses to clear a repo whose prose has no
# vault note and prints `fonde consign` as the remedy, so the incentive is to
# deposit everything and stop: that satisfies `fauche`, satisfies nothing else,
# and leaves the repo exactly as prose-heavy as before.
#
# scheduler#86 asks for that half to be "countable and prompted, not
# remembered", and names this shape first:
#
#     1. `fonde consign --report` / a `consigne status` that lists DUPLICATED
#        files, so the second half is visible as a queue rather than as nothing.
#
# `consigne status` is that. It is the whole reason this is a new door and not
# a rename: the deposit half already worked.
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
# ===========================================================================
#
# IT DELETES NOTHING, EVER -- not even a file it has just reported DUPLICATED.
# Removing a source file is the judgement `consign-prose.sh` declines to make
# and this declines for the same reason: "does this paragraph describe a
# premise that still holds?" (PROSE-REAPING.md 1) is not answerable by hashing.
# What changes is that the queue is now countable, so the judgement has
# somewhere to be applied instead of being remembered.
#
# IT NEVER PUSHES, and it never commits either: the deposit mechanism writes
# files into the vault working tree and stops. No code path here runs
# `git push` or `git commit`.
#
# THE REASON CHANGED ON 2026-08-12 EVEN THOUGH THE BEHAVIOUR DID NOT, which is
# worth ten seconds of a reader's time. It used to say the vault "is
# deliberately left unpushed (Zach's 2026-08-06 licensing/IP note)". That is
# reversed: the private hf7y/ecosystem1-vault remote IS the vault now, and a
# reaping pass commits AND pushes (PROSE-REAPING.md 2 and 5.6). What that rule
# actually cost, before it was reversed: mandark's working copy was found 19
# commits ahead of origin with 9 uncommitted paths -- one laptop holding the
# only copy of every consignment since 2026-08-02.
#
# So this tool still not committing is now a division of labour rather than a
# prohibition: depositing is mechanical, deciding what a commit SAYS is not.
#
# WHAT THIS TOOL DOES SAY, since hf7y/realisateur#212: whether the clone it
# just wrote into is actually ahead of origin or has uncommitted paths. It
# still commits and pushes nothing itself -- that division of labour did not
# move -- but a deposit that landed in a clone nobody then pushed used to be
# silent about it, which is exactly the mandark failure above, undetected
# until someone happened to look. Both `status` and a kept (0) plain deposit
# now end with that line when the clone is not clean; a clean clone stays
# quiet, same as `status`'s own "nothing to report" line does.
#
# exit: 0 kept  2 usage  5 broken  6 blind  7 refused
#       -- byte-identical to `fonde`'s own VERB_EXITS, because for the deposit
#       path they are literally the same process's exit code, unwrapped.

set -uo pipefail

SELF_NAME='consigne'

# ---- config, read here and nowhere else ------------------------------------
# THE SAME VARIABLE AND THE SAME DEFAULT AS `fonde`, deliberately. This is the
# "config read from one source, not retyped per file" row of BUILD-DISCIPLINE
# applied across a project boundary: a caller who has exported
# BIBLIOTHECAIRE_VAULT to point the old door somewhere must not have to learn
# a second name to point the new one at the same place, and a `consigne` that
# invented CONSIGNE_VAULT would deposit into a different tree than
# `fonde consign` for the same caller, silently.
# THE DEFAULT MOVED, 2026-08-12, Zach-directed ("vault moves off of any local
# host"): out of one user's home on one laptop and onto the FHS location for
# system-served data. A local clone is a CACHE of the GitHub remote, which is
# the vault; /srv is where a cache like that belongs, and it is readable by
# every account on the host rather than by whoever owns the home directory.
VAULT_DEFAULT=/srv/ecosystem1-vault
VAULT="${BIBLIOTHECAIRE_VAULT:-$VAULT_DEFAULT}"   # --vault overrides, below

die()    { printf '%s: %s\n' "$SELF_NAME" "$*" >&2; exit 2; }
blind()  { printf '%s: BLIND: %s\n' "$SELF_NAME" "$*" >&2; exit 6; }

# ---- the lock: serializing the vault's 13 writers --------------------------
# hf7y/realisateur#213: /srv/ecosystem1-vault is one working tree, writable by
# all thirteen self-dev accounts, with nothing serializing a deposit against
# another account's `git add`/`commit`/`pull` on the same checkout. flock on a
# file inside the vault itself -- the shape `usage-paced-runner.sh` already
# uses for its own run lock, not a new pattern. The lock file lives IN the
# vault (not $HOME) so it is shared by every writer, and the vault's own
# setgid 2775 dir makes a freshly-created lock file group `vault` and
# group-writable automatically -- no chmod step to forget.
# 66 is arbitrary but distinct from every code a wrapped command could
# plausibly reuse to mean something else -- a lock TIMEOUT must never be
# mistaken for the wrapped command's own exit 1.
CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT="${CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT:-30}"
CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT_RC=66
vault_locked() { # <vault> <command> [args...] -- runs command holding the lock
  local vault="$1"; shift
  [ -d "$vault" ] || blind "no vault at $vault to lock (set BIBLIOTHECAIRE_VAULT)"
  # NO `--` HERE: unlike most GNU tools, flock's own command-variant syntax is
  # `flock [options] <file> <command> [args...]` with no separator -- a `--`
  # in that position is parsed as the command itself and fails to execve.
  flock -w "$CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT" -E "$CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT_RC" \
    "$vault/.consigne.lock" "$@"
  local rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -eq "$CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT_RC" ]; then
    printf '%s: BLIND: another writer held the vault lock (%s/.consigne.lock) for %ss -- not proceeding\n' \
      "$SELF_NAME" "$vault" "$CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT" >&2
  fi
  return "$rc"
}

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
consigne -- deposit prose into the Obsidian vault, and report what a deposit
            left behind.

usage:
  consigne <path>...              deposit each path into the vault as linked
                                  data. Identical to `fonde consign <path>...`
                                  -- same mechanism, same deposit, same codes.
  consigne status [<project>...]  what is deposited AND still in the repo.
                                  No argument: every project in the vault.
  consigne lock -- <command>...   run <command> holding the vault's write
                                  lock (hf7y/realisateur#213). For the manual
                                  commit-and-push PROSE-REAPING.md 5.6 asks
                                  for -- mutually exclusive with every
                                  deposit and every other `lock` caller on
                                  the same vault. Exits with <command>'s own
                                  code, or 66 if the lock could not be
                                  acquired within CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT
                                  seconds (default 30).
  consigne --help                 this text

flags:
  --vault PATH  the vault to deposit into, for this invocation only. Beats
                $BIBLIOTHECAIRE_VAULT, which beats /srv/ecosystem1-vault.
                May appear anywhere before the `--` -- `--vault PATH lock --`
                and `lock --vault PATH --` are the same invocation.
  --summon      accepted and buys nothing. The deposit is mechanized and
                reaches no agent, so this spends zero and says so -- exactly
                as `fonde consign --summon` does, which is the remedy `fauche`
                prints for unconsigned prose.
  -h, --help    this text

exit:
  0  kept     -- deposited, or status read cleanly
  2  usage    -- bad arguments, or a path that does not exist
  5  broken   -- a note was written and read back with a different body
  6  blind    -- could not read the vault, a source, or the mechanism itself,
                 or another writer held the vault lock too long
  7  refused  -- would have overwritten an existing note, or the vault is not
                 under version control

  `consigne lock` is the one exception: it exits with its command's own
  code, or 66 (never 6) if the lock itself timed out -- a caller wrapping an
  arbitrary command needs a code that command cannot itself mean.

environment:
  BIBLIOTHECAIRE_VAULT   the vault (default /srv/ecosystem1-vault). The same
                         variable `fonde` and `fauche` read, on purpose: one
                         name, so pointing one door somewhere cannot leave
                         another pointing elsewhere. `--vault` beats it.
  CONSIGNE_IMPL          path to lib/consign-prose.sh, if it should not be
                         found automatically. Resolution order otherwise:
                         the installed `fonde`'s own tree, then `installe
                         list`'s row for `fonde`, then bibliothecaire's
                         checkout under $INSTALLE_PROJECTS.
  CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT  seconds to wait for the vault lock before giving up
                         (default 30). Applies to both a plain deposit and
                         `consigne lock`.

THIS TOOL DELETES NOTHING. `status` reports a queue; acting on it is a
judgement (PROSE-REAPING.md 1), and it is still yours to make.

NEVER PUSHES, NEVER COMMITS. It writes files and stops; the pass that ran it
commits and pushes (PROSE-REAPING.md 5.6). The remote IS the vault as of
2026-08-12 -- the older "deliberately left unpushed" rule is reversed.

see: man consigne; hf7y/scheduler#86 for the half `status` makes countable.
EOF
}

# ---- resolving the ONE implementation --------------------------------------
# Four candidates, tried in order, each PROBED rather than assumed -- a path
# that exists but is unreadable is not a resolution. If none answers, that is
# BLIND (6) and it is loud: a front door that silently did nothing because it
# could not find its own mechanism is the exit-0 no-op this estate keeps
# paying for.
#
# NOTE ON WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: no candidate here spells
# `verb-builds/current`. bin/lib/propagation-set.sh reserves resolving the pin
# path to the two layout-owning scripts, so this asks `fonde` and `installe`
# where they already are instead of re-deriving the layout they live in.
resolve_impl() {
  local c fonde_bin root

  if [ -n "${CONSIGNE_IMPL:-}" ]; then
    [ -r "$CONSIGNE_IMPL" ] \
      || blind "CONSIGNE_IMPL is set to $CONSIGNE_IMPL, which is not readable"
    printf '%s' "$CONSIGNE_IMPL"
    return 0
  fi

  # 1. The installed `fonde` carries it, one directory up from its own bin/.
  if fonde_bin="$(command -v fonde 2>/dev/null)" && [ -n "$fonde_bin" ]; then
    root="$(cd "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$fonde_bin")")/.." 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || root=''
    if [ -n "$root" ] && [ -r "$root/lib/consign-prose.sh" ]; then
      printf '%s' "$root/lib/consign-prose.sh"
      return 0
    fi
  fi

  # 2. `installe list` knows where every verb resolves to, including on a host
  #    whose PATH has not been re-exported since the last install.
  if command -v installe >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r n p; do
      [ "$n" = fonde ] || continue
      [ -n "$p" ] || continue
      root="$(cd "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$p")")/.." 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || continue
      [ -r "$root/lib/consign-prose.sh" ] || continue
      printf '%s' "$root/lib/consign-prose.sh"
      return 0
    done < <(installe list 2>/dev/null)
  fi

  # 3. A development checkout of bibliothecaire, for a host with no build.
  c="${INSTALLE_PROJECTS:-$HOME/Documents/Projects}/bibliothecaire/lib/consign-prose.sh"
  if [ -r "$c" ]; then
    printf '%s' "$c"
    return 0
  fi

  blind "cannot find lib/consign-prose.sh -- the deposit mechanism lives in
       bibliothecaire and is reached through the installed \`fonde\`, through
       \`installe list\`, or through a bibliothecaire checkout under
       \${INSTALLE_PROJECTS:-\$HOME/Documents/Projects}. None answered. Set
       CONSIGNE_IMPL to point at it directly, or install the verb build.
       Nothing was deposited."
}

# ---- push status: is the clone actually the vault? -------------------------
# hf7y/realisateur#212. The remote IS the vault as of 2026-08-12; a local
# clone is a cache, and nothing said so when the cache and the remote
# disagreed. Measured failure mode: mandark's clone sat 19 commits ahead of
# origin with 9 uncommitted paths, silently -- every consignment since
# 2026-08-02 existing in exactly one place, a laptop.
#
# Offline, like hygiene-lint.sh's own branch-unpushed check this mirrors: an
# ahead-count against the LAST FETCH, not re-fetched here, so it can go stale
# in the other direction (looking clean when origin has since moved). That is
# an acceptable false-negative for a check that costs one `git status`, not a
# CI gate -- the ecosystem's other ahead-count check makes the same trade for
# the same reason.
vault_push_report() { # <vault> -- prints one line to stderr if not clean, silent if it is
  local vault="$1" dirty=0 ahead=0 up
  git -C "$vault" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0

  dirty="$(git -C "$vault" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
  case "$dirty" in ''|*[!0-9]*) dirty=0 ;; esac

  up="$(git -C "$vault" rev-parse --abbrev-ref --symbolic-full-name '@{u}' 2>/dev/null)"
  if [ -n "$up" ]; then
    ahead="$(git -C "$vault" rev-list --count "$up..HEAD" 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$ahead" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ahead=0 ;; esac
  fi

  if [ "$dirty" -gt 0 ] || [ "$ahead" -gt 0 ]; then
    printf 'consigne: NOT PUSHED -- %s ahead of %s, %s uncommitted path(s) in %s\n' \
      "$ahead" "${up:-no upstream}" "$dirty" "$vault"
    printf '          this deposit is not in the vault until it is committed and pushed\n'
    printf '          (offline count against the last fetch, not re-fetched here)\n'
  fi
}

# ---- status: the half nothing consumed -------------------------------------
# Reads the vault's own notes, not a list. Every note written by
# consign-prose.sh carries the three fields this needs in its frontmatter --
# source_repo, source_path, source_sha256 -- so the comparison is against what
# the deposit ITSELF recorded, and a note this cannot classify is reported as
# UNREADABLE rather than counted as clean.
#
#   DUPLICATED  the source file is still there and still byte-identical.
#               The reaping pass deposited and stopped. This is the queue.
#   DIVERGED    still there, and no longer the same bytes. Worse than
#               DUPLICATED: the vault note is now a stale fork of a file
#               somebody has since edited, and neither copy says so.
#               scheduler#86 measured BLOCKERS.md already in this state.
#   REAPED      the source file is gone. The pass finished. Nothing to do.
#   UNREADABLE  the note has no usable provenance, or its source repo is not
#               on this host. Named, never silently skipped.
status_field() { # <file> <field> -- first frontmatter value, or empty
  sed -n "1,60{s/^$2: //p;}" "$1" 2>/dev/null | head -1
}

do_status() {
  [ -d "$VAULT" ] || blind "no vault at $VAULT (set BIBLIOTHECAIRE_VAULT)"

  vault_push_report "$VAULT"

  local -a projects=()
  if [ $# -gt 0 ]; then
    projects=("$@")
  else
    local d
    for d in "$VAULT"/*/; do
      [ -d "$d" ] || continue
      d="${d%/}"; d="${d##*/}"
      case "$d" in .*) continue ;; esac
      projects+=("$d")
    done
  fi
  [ "${#projects[@]}" -gt 0 ] && [ -n "${projects[0]}" ] \
    || blind "no project directories under $VAULT -- nothing has been consigned here"

  printf 'consigne status -- %s\n\n' "$VAULT"

  local n_dup=0 n_div=0 n_reap=0 n_bad=0 lines_dup=0

  # ARCHIVED REPOS ARE COLLAPSED, NOT DROPPED. On mandark most vault notes name
  # a source repo that is no longer on the host -- crt, chezz, basheur and
  # abletim alone account for hundreds. Printing one line each buries the
  # handful of DUPLICATED rows that are the entire point of this report, which
  # is how a report stops being read; and dropping them silently would be the
  # BLIND-graded-as-clean conflation this estate keeps paying for. So each
  # missing repo is named once, with its count, after the actionable rows.
  local -a gone_repo=() gone_n=()
  note_gone() { # <repo>
    local i
    for i in "${!gone_repo[@]}"; do
      if [ "${gone_repo[$i]}" = "$1" ]; then
        gone_n[i]=$(( ${gone_n[$i]} + 1 )); return 0
      fi
    done
    gone_repo+=("$1"); gone_n+=(1)
  }

  local proj note repo rel want got live n
  for proj in "${projects[@]}"; do
    if [ ! -d "$VAULT/$proj" ]; then
      printf '  UNREADABLE  %s -- no such directory in the vault\n' "$proj"
      n_bad=$((n_bad + 1))
      continue
    fi
    while IFS= read -r note; do
      [ -n "$note" ] || continue
      repo="$(status_field "$note" source_repo)"
      rel="$(status_field "$note"  source_path)"
      want="$(status_field "$note" source_sha256)"
      if [ -z "$repo" ] || [ -z "$rel" ] || [ -z "$want" ]; then
        printf '  UNREADABLE  %s -- no source_repo/source_path/source_sha256 frontmatter\n' \
          "${note#"$VAULT"/}"
        n_bad=$((n_bad + 1)); continue
      fi
      live="$repo/$rel"
      if [ ! -d "$repo" ]; then
        note_gone "$repo"
        n_bad=$((n_bad + 1)); continue
      fi
      if [ ! -f "$live" ]; then
        n_reap=$((n_reap + 1)); continue
      fi
      got="$(sha256sum "$live" 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1)"
      n="$(wc -l < "$live" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')"
      case "$n" in ''|*[!0-9]*) n=0 ;; esac
      if [ "$got" = "$want" ]; then
        printf '  DUPLICATED  %-52s (%s lines)\n' "$proj/$rel" "$n"
        n_dup=$((n_dup + 1)); lines_dup=$((lines_dup + n))
      else
        printf '  DIVERGED    %-52s -- the vault copy is a stale fork\n' "$proj/$rel"
        n_div=$((n_div + 1))
      fi
    done < <(find "$VAULT/$proj" -type f -name '*.md' 2>/dev/null | sort)
  done

  local i
  for i in "${!gone_repo[@]}"; do
    printf '  UNREADABLE  %s note(s) whose source repo is not on this host: %s\n' \
      "${gone_n[$i]}" "${gone_repo[$i]}"
  done

  printf '\n  %d DUPLICATED (%d lines in both places), %d DIVERGED, %d REAPED, %d UNREADABLE\n' \
    "$n_dup" "$lines_dup" "$n_div" "$n_reap" "$n_bad"

  # THE PROMPT. scheduler#86 asked for this half to be "countable AND
  # prompted". The count above is the countable half; this is the prompt, and
  # it names the judgement rather than offering to make it -- there is no
  # --remove flag here and there is not going to be one.
  if [ "$n_dup" -gt 0 ] || [ "$n_div" -gt 0 ]; then
    printf '\n  These are deposited and STILL IN THE REPO. A deposit is half a reaping\n'
    printf '  pass; the other half is per-file and is a judgement -- PROSE-REAPING.md 1:\n'
    printf '  "does this paragraph describe a premise that STILL HOLDS?". Nothing here\n'
    printf '  removes anything. A DIVERGED row is the more urgent one: the vault note\n'
    printf '  is already a stale fork and neither copy says so.\n'
  else
    printf '\n  Nothing is sitting in both places. (A REAPED row means the deposit was\n'
    printf '  made and the source is gone -- the pass finished.)\n'
  fi
  return 0
}

# ---- dispatch ---------------------------------------------------------------

# --vault MAY PRECEDE THE SUBCOMMAND, not just follow it (hf7y/realisateur#338).
# `consigne --vault PATH lock -- cmd` and `consigne lock --vault PATH -- cmd`
# must resolve to the same vault: --vault applies to the whole invocation
# regardless of what comes after it, so it is stripped out here, before
# anything -- including the `lock` dispatch below -- looks at argv position.
# Only consumed ahead of the first `--`, so a value a wrapped command passes
# after its own `--` is never touched. Config read from one source
# (BUILD-DISCIPLINE.md): this is now the only place `--vault`/`--vault=PATH`
# is parsed, for every form.
_pre=()
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --vault)   shift; [ $# -gt 0 ] || die "--vault needs a path"; VAULT="$1"; shift ;;
    --vault=*) VAULT="${1#*=}"; [ -n "$VAULT" ] || die "--vault needs a path"; shift ;;
    --) break ;;
    *) _pre+=("$1"); shift ;;
  esac
done
set -- "${_pre[@]}" "$@"

# `consigne lock -- <command> [args...]` -- hf7y/realisateur#213's second half.
# The deposit path below is locked automatically; PROSE-REAPING.md 5.6's manual
# `git commit`/`git push` into the shared vault is not automatic, and never
# will be (case F pins that this file runs neither) -- so it gets a door
# instead: a reaping pass wraps its own commit-and-push in this, and it is
# mutually exclusive with every deposit AND every other `lock` caller, because
# both hold the identical `$VAULT/.consigne.lock`. Handled before the general
# flag loop because `--` is otherwise rejected outright, below, and a wrapped
# command's own flags (`git commit -m ...`) must reach it unmangled.
if [ "${1:-}" = lock ]; then
  shift
  if [ $# -gt 0 ] && [ "$1" != "--" ]; then
    die "consigne lock: unexpected argument before '--': $1 (try --help)"
  fi
  [ "${1:-}" = "--" ] || die "consigne lock needs a command after '--' (try --help)"
  shift
  [ $# -gt 0 ] || die "consigne lock -- needs a command to run"
  vault_locked "$VAULT" "$@"
  exit $?
fi

SUMMON=0
ARGS=()
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
    --summon)  SUMMON=1 ;;
    # Highest-precedence form of the one config this tool has. Both spellings,
    # because `--vault=/x` silently becoming a positional path argument would
    # deposit the vault path itself as a document.
    --vault)   shift; [ $# -gt 0 ] || die "--vault needs a path"; VAULT="$1" ;;
    --vault=*) VAULT="${1#*=}"; [ -n "$VAULT" ] || die "--vault needs a path" ;;
    # A near-miss on the only flag that has ever meant "may spend money" must
    # fail rather than be swallowed as an unknown -- bin/lib/cli-guard.sh's
    # rule, restated here because a verb-shaped front door does not source it.
    -s|-S)     die "'$1' rejected as a near-miss on --summon (the cost flag is long-form only)." ;;
    --)        die "'--' is not accepted." ;;
    -*)        die "unknown flag: $1 (try --help)" ;;
    *)         ARGS+=("$1") ;;
  esac
  shift
done

if [ "${#ARGS[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
  die "needs at least one path, or \`status\` (try --help)"
fi

if [ "${ARGS[0]}" = status ]; then
  [ "$SUMMON" -eq 0 ] \
    || die "status never spends, so --summon requests nothing here."
  do_status "${ARGS[@]:1}"
  exit $?
fi

# ---- the deposit: forwarded, not reimplemented ------------------------------
IMPL="$(resolve_impl)" || exit $?

# `--summon` is accepted, buys nothing, and SAYS so rather than passing in
# silence -- the same sentence `fonde consign --summon` prints, for the same
# reason. `fauche` names that command as the remedy for unconsigned prose, so
# the flag must not have become a usage error the day the work it authorised
# became free.
[ "$SUMMON" -eq 1 ] && printf '%s: the deposit is mechanized; --summon buys nothing here and nothing was spent.\n' \
  "$SELF_NAME" >&2

# Foreground, output unswallowed. The impl prints each DEPOSITED path and
# what is now safe to remove, and that report is the caller's whole basis for
# deciding anything afterwards. Capturing IT (stdout) to inspect the exit code
# first is how it went missing -- so this still doesn't. The exit code alone
# is held one command longer than necessary so a kept (0) deposit can be
# followed by the #212 push-status line: a deposit is not IN the vault until
# it is committed and pushed, and nothing said so before this.
#
# LOCKED (hf7y/realisateur#213): thirteen accounts share this vault, so the
# write is serialized against every other deposit AND against a `consigne
# lock -- git commit ...` from PROSE-REAPING.md 5.6, above. A lock timeout
# is folded into the published `6 blind` -- this front door has never
# published a code for "another writer has it", and both mean the same thing
# to a caller: nothing was deposited, try again.
vault_locked "$VAULT" bash "$IMPL" "$VAULT" "${ARGS[@]}"
rc=$?
[ "$rc" -eq "$CONSIGNE_LOCK_TIMEOUT_RC" ] && rc=6
[ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && vault_push_report "$VAULT" >&2
exit "$rc"
